    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "And of course it is not. It is an insurance program easily identified by looking at your pay stub that says FICA, federal insurance contribution. Who's yours? And the American public understand this and knows, but we haven't made the kind of adjustments that need to be made. And we have more than 5 million Americans who have worked hard all their lives paid into the system, but receive a below poverty level check from Social Security. Our goal is to enhance the program, make it actually sound, which is critical to its success now and in the future. but also expand this so that nobody who works all their lives depending on their job. In most cases, it affects women and specifically women of color and black males who have been hurt the most by this. And we're out to correct it. John Lewis would say when he was on the committee, God rest his soul that this is the next major civil rights issue because of the way the program has discriminated. And what it would require is just the changes in the law that will expand Social Security benefits that will make sure that no one can retire into poverty and make sure, of course, that we're enhancing those benefits to individuals in a very modest way. No one's going to get wealthy on Social Security, but everybody should be receiving above poverty level checks so they can survive today.",
