    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/7/87450.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 103,
    "turn_text": "Well, Joe Biden prides himself on extending a hand across the aisle, as he puts it to his Republican opponents. But he hasn't extended much of a hand to Bernie Sanders, who certainly got far more votes in the primaries until his base collapsed when Biden won South Carolina. But up until South Carolina, Bernie had gotten far more votes by the American people than Joe Biden, who had a very limp campaign until then. And he has turned his back on Bernie. Bernie wants to be Secretary of Labor. That's clear. And Joe Biden's not going to appoint him, Secretary of Labor. The AFL-CIO is not supporting Bernie Sanders, who is on the picket lines before some of these guys were born, supporting labor unions. They're supporting some person from Massachusetts. And that leaves Bernie Sanders with a very powerful post if he is allowed to take it, which is chairman of the Senate Budget Committee.",
